body: "Follow-up to #165 (last fix round, round 5). Greptile's review of the round-5 head left two non-blocking findings. Each fix needs a test that fails on revert.\n\n1. [src/refusal.rs:48](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rust/pull/165#discussion_r4546851534): **URL redaction crosses URL boundaries.** `userinfos` turns `https://host.invalid:443/ x https://u2:secret2@two.invalid` into `https://[redacted]@two.invalid`: the port in the first URL lets the userinfo search run into the second URL. That over-redacts and leaks nothing, but it removes a public address from refusal fields and recovery arguments. Keep separate URLs separate (a userinfo span never contains whitespace), while still allowing nested schemes inside passwords. Must-allow: the example above keeps its first URL. Must-redact: `https://u:pa:ss@h` and a password containing `://`. The same class is reported upstream in unbraind/pm-cli#1707.\n2. [tests/support/workspace_unit.rs:605](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rust/pull/165#discussion_r4546851541): **Root test runs fail.** `round5_parent_index_io_modes` expects `PermissionDenied` after chmod 000, but root can still read the file. Apply the same root guard the nearby permission tests use.\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (7)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-12T14:46:28Z)\n>\n> Leftovers from pm-rust#165 round 7 (head 488dbe0). Each is oracle parity or policy, not a pm-rust divergence.\n> \n> 1. **Role-less close status.** With `workflow.close_status = done` and no terminal role on `done`, the published SDK admits a direct start and a stale ranked start of a closed item, and pm-rust matches it. Decide whether the configured close status alone should block a start. If so, change the oracle first. Thread: PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs62FkY8.\n> 2. **Dropped open-blocker warning.** The oracle drops its open-blocker warning on this path. Native behaviour follows the oracle until the oracle restores the warning.\n> 3. **Comment wrapper.** The native comment-wrapper output has a broader mismatch beyond the cases round 7 fixed.\n> \n> Evidence and the round's commands are in [docs/ORACLE_REVIEW_ROUND7_2026_11_12.md](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rust/blob/0d43438/docs/ORACLE_REVIEW_ROUND7_2026_11_12.md).\n> \n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-12T16:56:44Z)\n>\n> Round-eight oracle gap measured for #165 against the pinned `@unbrained/pm-cli` 2026.11.11, using synthetic non-git trackers.\n> \n> A persisted assignee contains:\n> \n> ```text\n> https://user:secret value://tail@host.invalid\n> ```\n> \n> The refusing invocation has no credential URL to enroll:\n> \n> ```sh\n> pm claim sample-own --author beta --json\n> ```\n> \n> The published CLI returns exit 4 with this unsafe field:\n> \n> ```text\n> claimed_by: https://user:secret value://[redacted]@host.invalid\n> ```\n> \n> Its `title` and `detail` also retain the outer password prefix. Native redacts the whole userinfo to `https://[redacted]@host.invalid`, including the human refusal stream. Refusals leave item and history unchanged.\n> \n> The comma and semicolon stored-holder cases match published JSON bytes. All three separators match complete published human/JSON streams when the credential URL is present in the invocation. The space-only stored-holder gap is frozen explicitly in `round8_stored_nested_password_projection`; the complete native JSON comparison replaces only the three leaking fields with fixed safe expectations. Existing differential assertions remain unchanged.\n> \n> Native correction: [e7b22b73086f49644dae51b9edce95c339884a3e](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rust/commit/e7b22b73086f49644dae51b9edce95c339884a3e). Regression coverage is in `src/refusal.rs` and `tests/claim_release_differential.rs`, with the intentional privacy divergence documented in `docs/NATIVE_PARITY_2026_11_11.md`. Whole whitespace-bearing userinfo remains private under the current contract. No upstream issue was filed.\n> \n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T00:21:59Z)\n>\n> Deferred from PR #165 review thread PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs621TkM (`src/orientation_deps.rs`, `project`): disclose missing-reference rows removed by the rendered delivery budget, excluding summary mode.\n> \n> The direct receipt correction is valid in isolation, but it changes the measured packet and violates the PR's mandatory pinned-oracle byte contract. With the existing `relationship_reference_limits_match_oracle` fixture (`deps sample-root --format context --edge-limit 40 --token-budget 300`), adding the bounds omission changes the advertised feasible recovery budget from the published 412 to native 434 tokens. The unchanged differential fails on those recovery bytes. Preserving 412 despite the larger corrected packet would advertise an infeasible retry.\n> \n> The new `round10_shortened_reference_receipt_matches_published_gap` comparison confirms the published gap directly: a successful full packet can deliver fewer missing references than `missing_reference_count`, with `context.meta.truncated=false` and `omission_receipt.has_omissions=false`. It compares complete stdout/stderr/status against the pinned 2026.11.11 CLI and includes complete-reference and summary controls. Native assertions still run when the optional oracle is absent.\n> \n> This needs a coordinated receipt, measurement and recovery-envelope contract change rather than an isolated native receipt patch. The candidate correction was withdrawn; existing differential assertions and thresholds are unchanged. No test is skipped or ignored. Retained parity and the original isolated failure are recorded on pm-rust-oracle-1111 for orchestrator follow-up.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T09:24:07Z)\n>\n> Round 14 evidence for PR #165, review thread PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63A9qa:\n> \n> On a fresh non-git Issue tracker, published pm-cli 2026.11.11 and native both refuse `close sample-one --actual-result \" \" --validate-close strict --json -- Done` with exit 2 and byte-identical refusal envelopes. Neither emits `recovery.suggested_retry` or `suggested_retry_args`; both emit `next_best_command` beginning `pm update sample-one --resolution \"<value>\" --expected-result \"<value>\" --actual-result \"<value>\"`. The existing ambiguous-close policy withdraws the retry before the reported insertion branch can run. The new `round14_blank_evidence_retry_matches_published_policy` differential pins this exact command without normalizing fields.\n> \n> Moving replacement flags in advice emitted for other delimiter cases would change published recovery bytes, so this minor is deferred here pending an upstream policy change. The reported reproducer does not emit the problematic retry. While executing the offered two-step advice, native update also refused the dedicated `--resolution` flag; generic `--set` supports fields, but the offered command itself needs a separate compatibility fix. No review thread was replied to or resolved.\n> \n> The dedicated update gap is independently confirmed: `update sample-one --resolution Complete --expected-result Expected --actual-result Verified --author alpha --json` exits 0 in the published CLI and 2 in native (`unexpected argument --resolution`). Future work is tracked by the open, unclaimed pm item `pm-rust-oracle-recovery-167`, tagged `gh:unbraind/pm-rust#167`.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T17:08:37Z)\n>\n> Deferred from pm-rust#165's review at 7d9d2bc, thread PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63MyrR (`src/selection.rs:508`). When no configured status has the `active` role, `candidates` can skip an unclaimed `open` item, because it does not use the registry's `active_for_blocking` fallback, so `claim_ranked` returns `no_available_next_item`. **Expected:** run the pinned pm-cli oracle with a schema in which no status carries `active`, then make native candidate selection match it (`claim --next`, `claim_ranked`), with an oracle differential.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T18:32:08Z)\n>\n> Deferred from pm-rust#165's review at c5df80e (rounds 17–18). These five threads are a class follow-up: #165 now redacts every RFC 3986-valid userinfo form plus the documented decorated forms, and the remaining findings are malformed-URL hardening and oracle-parity edges.\n> \n> 1. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63Oq5t (Major, privacy), `src/refusal.rs:149`.** In `https://u:abc@def/secret@host.invalid` the password contains an unencoded `@` and `/`. RFC 3986 requires both to be percent-encoded inside userinfo, so this is not a valid URL. Native ends userinfo at the last `@` before the first `/`, so only `abc` is registered, and copied text keeps `@def/secret`. **Expected:** one tokenizer for malformed authorities that treats everything up to the last `@` before the host boundary as userinfo, sharing a corpus with the existing redaction cases. Must still redact all RFC-valid forms and keep `user@example.invalid` emails verbatim.\n> 2. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63Oq5j (Minor, parity), `src/ownership.rs:511`.** The published pm-cli 2026.11.11 releases a freshly acquired claim when a late blocker refuses an explicit start and the item had no previous assignee. Native keeps the claim. Match the oracle with a differential.\n> 3. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63Oq5y (Minor), `src/workspace.rs:257`.** In admitted mode, a blocker file whose stored `id` duplicates the target id is indexed first and makes the target ambiguous (`ItemNotFound`). Check that the stored id matches the filename before indexing, as the oracle does.\n> 4. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63Oq54 (Trivial), `tests/claim_release_differential.rs:2010`.** The no-write snapshots exclude `runtime/transactions`, so a refusal that leaves a prepared claim journal would pass. Include the journals in the snapshots.\n> 5. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63Oq59 (Trivial), `tests/support/admission_unit.rs:1704`.** `configured = false` still has `schema.files.workflows` set from the fixture. Remove the key so the implicit-file merge path is exercised.\n> \n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T21:57:26Z)\n>\n> 6. **PRRT_kwDOTwgMRs63TUo5 (Minor), `src/lifecycle_admission.rs:279`.** After an id-prefix change to `new-`, `old-gate` and `new-old-gate` can both exist. If `blocked_by` names the open `old-gate` while a dependency edge names the closed `new-old-gate`, the recovery-advice normalization maps both to `new-old-gate` and suggests removing the wrong dependency. **Expected:** normalize only ids that resolve to the same item, as `blocker()` does, and add a differential for the prefix-migration case."
